  "N19" [label="(13,0,0) Currently, some lung cancer risk model training schemes require costly bounding-box annotations, making large-scale training datasets infeasible."];
  "N20" [label="(0,0,0) If a modeling approach requires costly expert annotations, then scaling to large real-world datasets becomes difficult, hindering broad deployment. (callable rule)"];
  "N21" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Annotation-heavy schemes are hard to scale; current risk models need costly bounding boxes; therefore current risk-model training is a scalability bottleneck for large-scale LCS AI."];
  "N25" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-knowledge: If a problem needs unified handling of multiple modalities and multiple tasks, then adopting a foundation-model paradigm is a plausible solution direction. (callable explanatory hypothesis)"];
  "N34" [label="(0,0,0) induction-common: If multiple independent obstacles (data curation difficulty, architecture/compute constraints) prevent existing FMs from working in a domain, then a domain-specific solution must address both dataset curation/alignment and scalable architecture/training. (callable generalization rule)"];
  "N35" [label="(0,0,0) induction-case: Currently, for LCS, existing FMs fail and the cited obstacles include data curation difficulty and architecture/compute constraints for 3D CT."];
  "N37" [label="(23,0,0) Currently, this work presents an integrated scalable data curation approach aligning high-dimensional images with other clinical datasets for LCS, covering 17 tasks and 49 data elements."];
